# This file is Stage 1 (`extract`) input. `extract` reads it and writes one
# claims/<memo_id>.md per memo; `build` then reads only those claims files,
# never this file. See docs/pipeline-overview.md.
#
# Memos to build the golden set for. Each memo needs:
#   id             - a stable identifier, carried through to every output row
#   source_folder  - a folder of PDFs; every PDF in it applies to every
#                    section below (no per-section source scoping)
#   sections       - a mapping of section name -> section text (the
#                    human-written memo text for that section). If a
#                    section has headings that name things its claims
#                    refer to, use a literal block scalar (`|`) or keep a
#                    blank line after each heading - a folded `>` scalar
#                    with no blank line runs the heading into the next
#                    sentence.
#
# A memo may also set any of these three optional tuning knobs, which
# apply to that memo's sections only. Omit one and it uses whatever the
# run passes (today: 0.3 / 5 / 40). Corpora seen so far range from about
# 1,000 to 6-7,000 chunks per memo, which is too wide a spread for one
# setting to suit every memo - a bigger corpus generally wants a higher
# relative_threshold and a smaller batch_size to keep cost and runtime
# down:
#   relative_threshold - 0.0 to 1.0; how close to the best-matching
#                        snippet a snippet must score to be checked at
#                        all. Higher = fewer candidates, cheaper run.
#   min_candidates     - integer >= 0; how many snippets to check even
#                        when almost nothing clears the threshold.
#   batch_size         - integer >= 1; how many snippets go to the AI in
#                        one request.
#
# Anything else written on a memo is an error, not ignored - so a typo
# like `batchsize: 25` is reported instead of silently doing nothing.
# Use a `#` comment for per-memo notes.
#
# This is a template. Copy it to `memos.yaml` (gitignored) and put the
# real memo text there - `memos.yaml` holds client memo sections, which
# are the deliverable and not committed, same as `.env` and `sources/`.
# The example below is made up (a fictional company, Acme); put its PDFs,
# or your own, in the source_folder to try the pipeline end to end.

memos:
  - id: MEMO-001
    source_folder: sources/acme
    sections:
      Business Profile: >
        Acme is the largest listed widget maker in Europe by plant value.
        Most of its sales are to carmakers, so demand was under significant
        pressure until Dec'23; it has stabilised as of Dec'24 versus Dec'23.
      Ownership: >
        Acme is a listed German industrial group with a market cap of
        EUR 4.2bn. Its free float is 60.5% of total outstanding shares.

    # Optional per-memo tuning (see the header). A larger corpus usually
    # wants a higher relative_threshold and smaller batch_size:
    # relative_threshold: 0.35
    # min_candidates: 8
    # batch_size: 25
